Package: flexCountReg
Type: Package
Title: Estimation of a Variety of Count Regression Models
Version: 0.1.2
Authors@R: c(person("Jonathan", "Wood", role = c("aut", "cre"),
                  email = "jwood2@iastate.edu", 
                  comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0003-0131-6384")),
           person("Guillermo", "Basulto-Elias", role = c("aut"),
                  email = "basulto@iastate.edu", 
                  comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-5205-2190")))
Maintainer: Jonathan Wood <jwood2@iastate.edu>
Description: Maximum likelihood and maximum simulated likelihood
    estimation of regression models for count data with flexible
    distributions. Twenty-eight count families are available, including
    the negative binomial (NB-1, NB-2, NB-P), generalized Poisson,
    Conway-Maxwell-Poisson, Poisson-lognormal, Poisson-inverse-Gaussian,
    Poisson-Lindley, Sichel, generalized Waring, Delaporte, Hermite,
    double Poisson, hyper-Poisson, discrete Weibull, gamma count and
    Polya-Aeppli distributions, with density, distribution, quantile and
    random-generation functions. Any distribution parameter can be modeled
    as a function of covariates, and models can include zero-inflated or
    hurdle components, underreporting of counts, random parameters (with
    heterogeneity in their means and variances), random-effects panel
    models (negative binomial, Poisson-Lindley and negative multinomial),
    and finite mixtures and latent classes of any of the families, with
    covariate-dependent class membership and one class per panel. Fitted
    models support predictions of expected counts and count
    probabilities, confidence, prediction and tolerance intervals,
    marginal effects and elasticities, likelihood-ratio tests and
    comparison tables, rootograms, randomized quantile residuals,
    cumulative residual (CURE) plots, and the 'broom' and 'tidymodels'
    interfaces. The forms of the negative binomial are described by
    Greene (2008) <doi:10.1016/j.econlet.2007.10.015>. For treatises on
    count models, see Cameron and Trivedi (2013)
    <doi:10.1017/CBO9781139013567> and Hilbe (2012)
    <doi:10.1017/CBO9780511973420>. For underreporting in count models,
    see Wood et al. (2016) <doi:10.1016/j.aap.2016.06.013>. For
    prediction methods in random parameter models, see Wood and Gayah
    (2025) <doi:10.1016/j.aap.2025.108147>. For estimating random
    parameters by maximum simulated likelihood, see Greene and Hill (2010)
    <doi:10.1108/S0731-9053(2010)26>, Gourieroux and Monfort (1996)
    <doi:10.1093/0198774753.001.0001> and Hensher et al. (2015)
    <doi:10.1017/CBO9781316136232>. For latent-class count regression, see
    Wedel et al. (1993) <doi:10.1002/jae.3950080404>; for randomized
    quantile residuals, Dunn and Smyth (1996)
    <doi:10.1080/10618600.1996.10474708>; and for rootograms, Kleiber and
    Zeileis (2016) <doi:10.1080/00031305.2016.1173590>.
